## What arrives from a generator

A mesh from an image-to-3D or text-to-3D generator is a BLOCKOUT. Treat
it as a silhouette proposal, not an asset.

What it reliably is: roughly the right shape, quickly. What it reliably
is not: manifold, budgeted, UV-mapped, or built from parts you can name
and edit. Native-mesh generators top out in the hundreds to a few
thousand faces; surface-extraction pipelines overshoot by orders of
magnitude in the other direction. Neither lands on a budget by accident.

## The lane

1. Import and MEASURE before deciding anything. Triangle count,
   component count, manifoldness, bounding box. The numbers decide the
   route, not the render.
2. Fix scale and orientation first. Everything measured afterwards is
   wrong if the object is at the wrong size or lying on its side.
3. Bounded cleanup only, with the threshold printed. See the repair
   caveat: an aggressive gap-close on generated geometry invents
   surfaces that were never proposed.
4. Then decide honestly which of three things this is:
   - a reference to model FROM, and you write the builder;
   - a background asset that survives cleanup as-is;
   - a base for retopology.
   Say which one you chose and why, in numbers.

## Cleanup technique

These are harness-side operations the writer does not call directly;
each carries the number that measured it.

- Weld BEFORE decimating: as-imported decimation tore 4,668 boundary
  edges open where weld-then-decimate left 0, with 4.6x less shading
  error.
- Cull invisible geometry before spending any decimation budget: 81%
  of one body's 26,756 triangles were buried, and removing them changed
  nothing visible. Probe by ray-casting along normals, not
  closest-point-plus-dot (read 9.7% covered where truth was 81.1%).
- Convert triangles to quads BEFORE decimating (79.6% quads first vs
  23.6% after); never use UNSUBDIV, which collapsed 79.6% to 33.5%.
- Retopology is a topology tool, not a reduction tool: remesh-then-
  decimate measured 0.1358 shading RMSE where weld-then-decimate
  measured 0.0293.
- Do not protect vertex-group regions during decimation: at a fixed
  triangle budget protection is zero-sum and measured worse (0.9889/
  0.0993 with all three protections vs 0.9953/0.0657 with none) — one
  body, one budget. Boundary error terms are NOT optional
  (10.1145/1061347.1061350): without them the core quadric metric
  degrades all open boundaries.

## What not to do

Do not decimate your way to a budget and call it done. Decimation on
non-manifold generated geometry tears holes and drags UVs with it, and
the tear is not visible from the angle you rendered.

Do not present a cleaned generation as if it were built. The user needs
to know which lane an asset came from, because the two carry different
guarantees about what can be changed later.